--TEST--
A `protected` property and a `protected` method are refused in a class that does not extend the one declaring them, and reached in a class that does — `rule:core-api/written-visibility`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:classes/declaring-a-class
// `protected` is the middle of the three levels, so it takes both classes to
// bound it: `Branch` extends `Ledger` and reads the same two members without
// a word from the checker, while `Report` merely holds one. Exactly two
// diagnostics is what says the subclass half passed.
class Ledger {
    protected int $balance = 0;

    protected function audit(): int {
        return $this->balance;
    }
}

class Branch extends Ledger {
    public function total(): int {
        return $this->balance + $this->audit();
    }
}

class Report {
    public function show(Ledger $ledger): int {
        int $seen = $ledger->balance;
        return $seen + $ledger->audit();
    }
}
echo "never reached", "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0471]: `Ledger::$balance` is `protected`, so only `Ledger` and the classes that extend it reach it
%A
error[E0471]: `Ledger::audit()` is `protected`, so only `Ledger` and the classes that extend it reach it
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
